WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.856
Your personal operating manual.

2
00:00:02.136 --> 00:00:12.013
The elective ends with a document only you can write: how you work with coding agents, written down so you follow it on busy days and revise it as tools change.

3
00:00:12.293 --> 00:00:13.467
Keep it to a page.

4
00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:17.267
Here’s a skeleton to fill in from your lab and project results.

5
00:00:17.547 --> 00:00:18.443
Defaults.

6
00:00:18.723 --> 00:00:25.251
Tool, model, and effort for everyday work, and when you change each (from lesson 3 and your own comparison).

7
00:00:25.531 --> 00:00:26.597
Before a task.

8
00:00:26.877 --> 00:00:30.099
Your delegation rule (the exercise above, adjusted).

9
00:00:30.379 --> 00:00:36.885
When you write a spec, when you use plan mode, what a good prompt for you includes (the symptom, the place, what done looks like).

10
00:00:37.165 --> 00:00:38.061
Context.

11
00:00:38.341 --> 00:00:39.685
What lives in your CLAUDE.

12
00:00:39.965 --> 00:00:41.288
md or AGENTS.

13
00:00:41.568 --> 00:00:43.509
md, and your pruning schedule.

14
00:00:43.789 --> 00:00:44.579
Checks.

15
00:00:44.859 --> 00:00:51.280
Which hooks run in your repos, what your Stop condition is, which paths are protected, what CI enforces.

16
00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:52.307
Review.

17
00:00:52.587 --> 00:00:57.493
Your checklist and reading order (lesson 8), and when you add a second-model review.

18
00:00:57.773 --> 00:00:58.755
Security.

19
00:00:59.035 --> 00:01:07.632
What the agent may never touch, which credentials it gets, when you use a container or the sandbox, and how you handle untrusted repositories.

20
00:01:07.912 --> 00:01:08.701
Learning.

21
00:01:08.981 --> 00:01:14.827
Which skills you’re deliberately keeping in your own hands this quarter, and which habits from the list above you’re using.

22
00:01:15.107 --> 00:01:15.981
Measurement.

23
00:01:16.261 --> 00:01:22.704
One number you’ll track (cost per merged change, rework minutes, defects found in review) and when you’ll look at it.

24
00:01:22.984 --> 00:01:29.149
Date it, keep it in your notes or your dotfiles, and revisit it whenever your tools change in a way that matters.

25
00:01:29.429 --> 00:01:34.080
A manual you haven’t revised in six months is describing a different set of tools.
